    {
      "source": "hn",
      "title": "GPT-6.2 Astra is the first model to beat Googlers' code golf records",
      "url": "https://bjin.github.io/go-codegolf-benchmark/#leaderboard",
      "author": "bjin",
      "external_id": "50601280",
      "summary": null,
      "why_read": null,
      "authors": [],
      "category": null,
      "published": "2026-11-22T16:44:48Z",
      "score": 443,
      "comments": 126,
      "hn_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50601280",
      "snippet": "Back in 2015, when I was still at Google, there was a small group of code-golf enthusiasts spending hours trying to save just one byte on their solutions for weekly code-golf problems. No matter how hard you tried, there always seemed to be another Googler who found a shorter solution. And once a problem entered the challenge phase and every solution became public, it was always astonishing to read how brilliant other people&#x27;s solutions were.<p>11 years later, LLMs are evolving fast. A recent OpenAI post (&quot;Perplexity and Intelligence&quot;, <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;openai.com&#x2F;index&#x2F;perplexity-and-intelligence&#x2F;\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;openai.com&#x2F;index&#x2F;perplexity-and-intelligence&#x2F;</a>) described researchers watching a model solve open math problems one after another in a Slack thread. I started to wonder: how would frontier models compete",
      "links": []
    },
